    {
      "id": "frq-6-4",
      "source": "Original",
      "title": "Quantitative Analysis: EROI and Radioactive Decay",
      "prompt": "A utility company is comparing two potential energy investments. Investment X requires 4 units of energy input to extract, process, and deliver 60 units of usable energy. Investment Y requires 5 units of energy input to deliver 45 units of usable energy. Separately, the utility must also plan long-term storage for 160 kg of a radioactive waste isotope with a half-life of 24 years. (a) Calculate the EROI for Investment X and for Investment Y, showing your work. (b) Identify which investment represents the more efficient net energy return, and justify your answer using your calculated EROI values. (c) Calculate how many kilograms of the radioactive isotope will remain after 72 years, showing your work. (d) Explain why the amount remaining after 72 years is not equal to zero, even though 72 years represents three full half-lives.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "label": "(a)",
          "ask": "Calculate EROI for both investments, showing work.",
          "steps": [
            "Investment X: EROI = Energy Obtained ÷ Energy Invested = 60 ÷ 4 = 15:1.",
            "Investment Y: EROI = Energy Obtained ÷ Energy Invested = 45 ÷ 5 = 9:1."
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "(b)",
          "ask": "Identify the more efficient investment and justify using the EROI values.",
          "steps": [
            "Investment X (EROI = 15:1) is the more efficient net energy investment, since it returns 15 units of usable energy for every 1 unit invested, compared to Investment Y's 9 units returned per unit invested — Investment X delivers substantially more net energy benefit per unit of energy input."
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "(c)",
          "ask": "Calculate the amount of isotope remaining after 72 years, showing work.",
          "steps": [
            "72 years ÷ 24-year half-life = 3 half-lives elapsed.",
            "N(t) = N0 x (1/2)^3 = 160 kg x 0.125 = 20 kg remaining."
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "(d)",
          "ask": "Explain why the remaining amount is not zero after 3 half-lives.",
          "steps": [
            "Radioactive decay is exponential, not linear: each half-life removes half of whatever quantity is still present at that point, not a fixed fraction of the original starting amount. After 3 half-lives, 160 kg x (1/2)^3 = 20 kg remains (12.5% of the original 160 kg) — the quantity approaches zero but, mathematically, never reaches exactly zero, which is why long-half-life isotopes require extended secure storage planning."
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
